## IV. The Default Condition of Labor: Monopsony and the Sand Trap

**The claim.** Monopsony is not a foreign object lodged in the free market; it is the market's historical default, and the competitive labor market is the exception — roughly 1935–1975 in the West, deliberately constructed and now visibly eroding. The trap is structural: markets self-regulate through negative feedback (exit disciplines the wage), but monopsony's entire operation is the suppression of exit — so the trap is built out of the confiscated escape ladder. Worse, it runs on positive feedback: suppressed wages prevent the savings mobility requires, deepening the power that suppresses the wages. Markets self-correct where feedback is negative; where feedback is positive they self-deepen.

**Two species, one refinement.** Rent-capture monopsony (can pay, won't — the Apple-Google no-poach cartel) versus solvency-preservation monopsony (can't pay, so coercion balances an equation the market is trying to declare unbalanceable — the post-plague landlord, the postbellum planter, the marginal company town). The second species is the more dangerous, because in competitive theory the employer who cannot pay the scarcity wage is supposed to dissolve so his capital and workers reallocate. Coercive monopsony suspends the verdict and keeps expired structures upright — the whole economy pays the carrying cost. Corollary (Habakkuk): scarcity that gets paid produces innovation (19th-c. American mechanization); scarcity that gets coerced produces preservation of the obsolete (the South's technological stagnation).

**The historical record of exits.** No labor market has ever competed its way out from the inside. Every escape was exogenous: plague, war, open frontier (the American frontier as continental wage floor), or organized political force (franchise expansion, Wagner Act, unions as coordination technology — the union is not interference with the exit mechanism; it *is* the exit mechanism, industrialized). Political correlation: the variable predicting monopsony intensity across regimes is not left/right but the state's posture toward **exit and voice**. Regimes that restrict mobility maximize it across ideological opposites (serfdom, apartheid pass laws, kafala, Soviet internal passports, the Nazi Arbeitsbuch of 1935); democratization episodes correlate with de-monopsonization (Master and Servant repeal follows the 1867 Reform Act; Wagner follows industrial labor's enfranchisement). Where workers acquire political voice, they convert it into market voice.

**Stagnation loop.** Stagnation breeds monopsony (zero-sum conditions shift the rational strategy from creating surplus to seizing structural control of shares) and monopsony breeds stagnation — because it disables both endogenous correctives at once: exit, which fixes prices, and entry, which fixes structures (the noncompete legally forbids the worker from becoming the disruptor). It is the one form of entrenchment that kills negative feedback and creative destruction with a single instrument.

**Sources.** Statute of Labourers (1351) and Ordinance of Labourers (1349) — the founding case: post-plague scarcity converted into a police matter. Suresh Naidu & Noam Yuchtman, "Coercive Contract Enforcement" (AER 2013) — Master and Servant prosecutions spiked when labor demand rose. Alan Manning, *Monopsony in Motion* — search frictions give every employer wage-setting power; perfect labor competition is a blackboard limit never reached. Card & Krueger (1994) minimum wage findings as the empirical crack. Azar, Marinescu & Steinbaum on labor market concentration. David Weil, *The Fissured Workplace*. Veena Dubal, "On Algorithmic Wage Discrimination" — the monopsony instrument without the company town; connect to the Algorithmic Citizen. J.K. Galbraith, *American Capitalism* — countervailing power. Mancur Olson, *The Rise and Decline of Nations* — distributional coalitions; Germany/Japan vs. Britain. Walter Scheidel, *The Great Leveler* — the four horsemen; nothing gentler has ever compressed inequality. Schumpeter, *Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy* — disequilibrium as engine; circular flow as economic death. H.J. Habakkuk, *American and British Technology in the Nineteenth Century*. Acemoglu & Johnson, *Power and Progress* — entrenched power steers innovation toward preserving itself; the shock generator captured. Albert Hirschman, *Exit, Voice, and Loyalty* — the master grammar; and *National Power and the Structure of Foreign Trade* (1945) — trade architecture as coercion, the diplomatic monopsony playbook (Germany and the Balkans). Philip Mirowski, *More Heat Than Light* — economics' theft of equilibrium from statics; a system at equilibrium is a system where nothing happens (Prigogine: living systems survive far from equilibrium).

