Testing the hypothesis with alternative AI that is more critical to explore what holds and what does not: 

I. The Causality Problem: You're Running Correlation Backward
Your core engine is: commodity → enclosure → citizen-form. But the evidence you marshal runs the other direction. You observe a citizen-form (say, the yeoman freeholder) and then identify the commodity underneath it. That's not causality — that's taxonomy with a causal paint job.
Consider: the first enclosure of land in England (16th–19th centuries) preceded the expansion of the franchise by centuries. If enclosure determines citizenship, why did the enclosed get the vote two hundred years later? The actual historical sequence looks more like: enclosure → dispossession → proletarianization → later political struggle → eventual franchise expansion. The citizen-form doesn't fall out of the commodity; it falls out of political contestation over the commodity. Your framework silently deletes the struggle.
Worse for your ladder: the "Sovereign Citizen" on land — when exactly was this? Harrington's Oceana (1656) was a utopia, not a description. The actual English freeholder was already being enclosed when Harrington wrote. You're treating a political-theoretical aspiration as a historical baseline. That's like using Plato's Republic to describe Athenian citizenship.
The real test: Can you point to a society where the same commodity substrate produced a different citizen-form? If not, your "determination" claim is unfalsifiable — every case confirms it because the categories are built to fit.

II. The Missing Rung: Capital
You jump from labor to data, skipping capital/financialization entirely. This is not innocent. Capital as the dominant commodity (roughly 1980–2010, the financialization era) doesn't fit your enclosure frame neatly — because financial capital isn't enclosed in the same way. It's abstract, mobile, and its power operates through access rather than exclusion. The hedge fund doesn't fence a commons; it trades claims on future enclosures.
This matters because the "Surveillance Citizen" on data sits awkwardly between labor and knowledge. Data extraction (Zuboff) looks more like a mutation of capital accumulation than a new commodity rung. Facebook doesn't enclose data the way a lord enclosed a field; it harvests behavioral exhaust that users didn't know was valuable. The enclosure metaphor strains here — there's no commons being taken away; there's a new commons being created (the platform) and then privatized. That's different.
Your ladder wants clean sequential rungs, but the actual economy is palimpsest: land, labor, capital, data, and knowledge all operate simultaneously, layered on top of each other. The American homeowner (land + debt instrument), the gig worker (labor + data exhaust), the AI researcher (knowledge worker + equity holder) — these are not citizens of different epochs. They're hybrid subjects of a layered economy. Your framework flattens what it should stratify.

III. The Monopsony "Default" Is Overstated
You claim monopsony is the historical default and competitive labor markets the exception (~1935–1975). But this depends on defining "competitive" impossibly narrowly. The 19th-century American frontier — the entire continental expansion — created genuine labor scarcity in vast regions. Wages in the Midwest exceeded European levels precisely because workers could exit to homesteads. That's not monopsony; that's a reservation wage set by an alternative mode of production (the Jeffersonian freehold you valorize).
Your response would be: the frontier was exogenous, not endogenous. But so was the Black Death (your founding case for monopsony relief). If exogenous shocks are the only escape, then the "default" isn't monopsony — it's contingency. The competitive market isn't the exception; it's one possible outcome among many, depending on institutional and geographical conditions.
More damaging: your 1935–1975 "exception" coincides with the most violent period in human history (World Wars, depression, Holocaust). If competitive labor markets require total war and economic collapse to emerge, your argument becomes: monopsony is the default in peacetime. That's a much weaker claim, and it points to institutional design (unions, regulation) rather than structural necessity.

IV. The Knowledge Commons You Mourn Was Historically Abnormal
Section VII presents the open knowledge commons (Royal Society, arXiv) as something invented that "can be de-invented." But the pre-1665 state — Tartaglia encrypting the cubic equation, guilds swearing secrecy — was the norm for most of human history. The Oldenburg bargain (priority for disclosure) was the exception, not the baseline. You're mourning the loss of a 350-year interlude as if it were the natural state.
This inverts your own logic. If knowledge has "always" been partially enclosed, then the knowledge economy isn't doing something unprecedented — it's reverting to type, with new tools. The "third enclosure" (physical AI extracting tacit knowledge) is alarming, but it's continuous with Taylorism and Braverman's "separation of conception from execution" — a 150-year trend, not a rupture.
Your ladder wants each rung to be a qualitative leap. But knowledge-as-commodity looks more like intensification than transcendence. The AI researcher is still selling labor (cognitive labor), just at a higher abstraction. The "Algorithmic Citizen" isn't a new species; she's a knowledge worker with worse bargaining power because her tools are owned by someone else. That's not a new citizen-form; that's the proletariat with a PhD.

V. Prometheus: You're Building Theory on Sand
You admit the $100B affiliated fund is "reported, not confirmed." But your case study depends on it. If that figure is venture-media inflation — and given that you're citing CNBC, Axios, TechCrunch in June 2026, this is entirely possible — then the "enclosure in the strict historical sense" collapses into a standard private-equity rollup with AI branding.
The deeper problem: even if the numbers are real, Prometheus doesn't enclose a commons. There is no "commons of distributed embodied know-how" in American manufacturing — there's fragmented, firm-specific tacit knowledge. What Prometheus is doing is consolidation and extraction, which is old-fashioned vertical integration with a neural network. The "commons" here is a rhetorical construction. You're calling something a commons so you can call its acquisition an enclosure.
Compare: the first enclosure took land that peasants actually used (grazed animals, gathered firewood). The third enclosure takes knowledge that workers have in their heads but that was never collectively governed. There's no "common" being privatized; there's individual human capital being appropriated. That's closer to slavery or indenture than to enclosure. Your framework blurs this because "enclosure" sounds more historically grounded than "extraction."

VI. The Marshall Reversal Ignores Expansions
You frame your work as "the fourth chapter Marshall did not live to write, and it is a reversal." But Marshall's story of expanding citizenship has continued in important domains: the 1965 Voting Rights Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, Obergefell (2015), the ACA's expansion of healthcare access. You don't have to think these are sufficient to notice that citizenship hasn't simply contracted — it has transformed, with losses and gains.
Your "Algorithmic Citizen" owns "none of the substrate of independence." But the same citizen has access to more information than any human in history, can organize collective action at scale (BLM, #MeToo, Arab Spring — however mixed their outcomes), and in many jurisdictions has stronger social rights than the "Natural Rights Citizen" of 1900. The substrate has changed, but independence isn't only ownership. Your framework assumes that political agency requires property in the self or its products — but that's Macpherson's assumption, not an empirical finding.
If a citizen can organize, speak, and vote without owning land, labor, data, or knowledge — and billions do — then your substrate theory needs defending, not asserting.

VII. The Corridor Is Wider Than You Admit
